  "meta": {
    "title": "Infinitely many a’s and infinitely many b’s",
    "blurb": "GF a ∧ GF b — the fairness condition of a scheduler that must serve two processes forever. Wait for an a, then for a b; each time both have happened, pass through F and start the round again.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "(ab)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "c(acb)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "aaa(ba)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "b(aacbcc)",
        "expect": "accept"
      },
      {
        "w": "(a)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "ab(c)",
        "expect": "reject"
      },
      {
        "w": "(bc)",
        "expect": "reject"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "ltl",
        "fairness",
        "intersection"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "readme": "Intersecting two Büchi conditions is not as simple as taking a product and requiring both F's: the two might each be visited infinitely often but never at the same time. The standard fix is a **round**: first wait for the first condition, then for the second, and only then count a visit to F. That is exactly this machine for GF a ∧ GF b.\n\nA word is accepted when its periodic part contains both an a and a b. **(ab)**, **c(acb)** and **aaa(ba)** are accepted; **(a)**, **ab(c)** and **(bc)** are not."
    }
  }
}
